<% content_for :title, "Principles" %>

<div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-5 py-12">
  <header class="border-b border-[--color-rule] pb-6 mb-10">
    <p class="text-sm uppercase tracking-wide text-[--color-ink-soft]">About</p>
    <h1 class="font-serif text-4xl text-[--color-ink] mt-1">Principles</h1>
    <p class="mt-3 italic text-[--color-ink-soft] font-serif">
      What guides this site, in three commitments.
    </p>
  </header>

  <article class="font-serif text-[--color-ink] leading-relaxed text-lg space-y-5 [&_p]:max-w-prose">

    <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-[--color-ink] mt-14 mb-4 first:mt-2">Free, in the strict sense</h2>

    <p>
      Every text in the corpus, every font, every line of source code, and
      every gem we depend on must meet the
      <a href="https://www.gnu.org/philosophy/free-sw.en.html" class="underline decoration-dotted hover:text-[--color-link]">FSF&rsquo;s definition of free software</a>
      &mdash; the four freedoms: the freedom to run, study, modify, and
      redistribute, with or without changes.
    </p>

    <p>
      Public-domain texts are welcome. Permissive licenses (CC BY, MIT,
      Apache, BSD) are welcome. <em>Copyleft</em> licenses (CC BY-SA, GPL,
      AGPL, the SIL Open Font License) are equally welcome &mdash; share-alike
      is just one of several ways to be free, and we have no preference
      between permissive and copyleft. What we do not accept: non-commercial
      clauses, no-derivatives clauses, custom &ldquo;used by permission&rdquo;
      EULAs, and any open-core dependency whose useful features sit behind
      a proprietary tier. If a source can&rsquo;t be redistributed by anyone
      who downloads it from us, it doesn&rsquo;t belong here.
    </p>

    <p>
      This is the reason a project this scope can exist at all: every
      contributor and every reader stands on the same legal footing as the
      maintainers. The corpus you read is the corpus you can fork.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-[--color-ink] mt-14 mb-4 first:mt-2">Academic consensus over dogmatic bias</h2>

    <p>
      The site is built for serious study. That means defaulting to
      mainstream critical scholarship and presenting the textual evidence
      honestly &mdash; not the tradition of any single church, school, or
      confession.
    </p>

    <p>
      Where translations and editions disagree, we show the disagreement
      rather than picking a side. Where critical editions reconstruct or
      bracket a verse, we follow the editor&rsquo;s notation rather than
      smoothing it into the body text. Where scholarship is divided, the
      reader sees the apparatus, not a verdict. The presentation is
      designed for the kind of reader who wants to <em>see for themselves</em>
      &mdash; whether they are a graduate student, a parish priest, a Talmud
      student, a textual critic, or someone reading the Bible for the first
      time and curious about the seams.
    </p>

    <p>
      We will sometimes have to make judgment calls about which witnesses
      to surface by default. Those defaults follow current consensus in
      the relevant field &mdash; for example, NA28 / ECM for the Greek New
      Testament, BHS for the Hebrew Bible, the Göttingen LXX or Rahlfs for
      the Septuagint &mdash; not the preference of any one tradition.
      Where we cannot host the consensus edition for legal reasons, we
      say so.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-[--color-ink] mt-14 mb-4 first:mt-2">Big tent &mdash; with a light hand</h2>

    <p>
      The corpus is meant to be inclusive. Anything an academic might
      reasonably want to read in connection with the biblical literature
      belongs here: not just the protestant 66, but the deuterocanonical
      and apocryphal books, the pseudepigrapha, the Dead Sea Scrolls,
      the Nag Hammadi library, the Apostolic Fathers, the Gnostic gospels,
      the rabbinic literature, and the historical commentary that has
      surrounded all of it for two millennia. Texts whose canonicity is
      contested in any tradition can still teach us about the texts that
      are not contested.
    </p>

    <p>
      Moderation exists, but with a light hand and a clear principle: we
      curate for <em>academic relevance</em>, not for theological agreement.
      The Gospel of Thomas belongs here. So do Philo, Josephus, Origen,
      Maimonides, Calvin, Fox, and Barclay. Texts that have no historical
      or scholarly relationship to the biblical literature do not.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-[--color-ink] mt-14 mb-4 first:mt-2">Customizable, with sensible defaults</h2>

    <p>
      Different readers want different things. A scholar comparing the
      Masoretic Text to the Septuagint wants both side by side with the
      apparatus visible. A parish priest reading the lectionary wants a
      clean modern translation with footnotes only when textual decisions
      affect meaning. A student of patristics wants Greek and Latin
      sources together with their English translations.
    </p>

    <p>
      The reader will let you choose, per account, which texts and
      commentaries appear by default in your parallel view, in your
      cross-reference panel, and in your search results &mdash; a kind of
      personal corpus inside the larger one. The defaults are chosen to
      be useful to a curious newcomer with no prior preference, but
      <em>nothing in the site is committed to those defaults except the
      defaults themselves</em>. Everything that can be customized will be.
    </p>

    <hr class="border-[--color-rule] my-12">

    <p class="text-sm text-[--color-ink-soft] italic">
      These principles are operating commitments, not platitudes. If you
      catch the site violating one of them &mdash; bundling a non-free
      dependency, hiding an inconvenient textual variant, refusing a text
      on doctrinal rather than scholarly grounds &mdash; please open an
      issue on
      <a href="https://git.sr.ht/~sethkush/openscriptorium" class="underline decoration-dotted hover:text-[--color-link]">the source repository</a>.
    </p>

    <p class="text-sm text-[--color-ink-soft] mt-6 not-italic">
      For scholars, editors, and lexicographers: please also read our
      <%= link_to "appeal", appeal_to_scholars_path,
            class: "underline decoration-dotted hover:text-[--color-link]" %>
      asking you to release your editorial work under a license that
      respects the four freedoms.
    </p>
  </article>
</div>
